Implement a production-quality AWS CloudWatch metric preprocessing module in Java 17/Spring Boot using AWS SDK for Java v2.

The purpose of the module is to prepare CloudWatch time-series data for a downstream LLM that performs trend analysis.

The preprocessing must support ANY analysis period.

Examples:

* 1 hour
* 6 hours
* 12 hours
* 24 hours
* 3 days
* 7 days
* 14 days
* 30 days

The preprocessing must NOT assume that the analysis is always daily.

==================================================

1. INPUT
    ==================================================

Input contains:

* analysisStart
* analysisEnd
* requested analysis duration
* AWS region
* service
* resource/dimensions
* metric name
* metric unit
* raw CloudWatch timestamps
* raw CloudWatch values
* metric aggregation type
* optional threshold configuration

Example:

{
“analysisStart”: “2026-08-27T00:00:00Z”,
“analysisEnd”: “2026-09-02T23:59:59Z”,
“region”: “ap-south-1”,
“metricName”: “5xx”,
“unit”: “Count”,
“aggregation”: “SUM”,
“dataPoints”: […]
}

==================================================
2. DYNAMIC GRANULARITY

Determine analysis bucket size from the requested analysis duration.

Use configurable defaults:

<= 1 hour:
5 minute buckets

1 hour and <= 6 hours:
15 minute buckets

6 hours and <= 24 hours:
1 hour buckets

1 day and <= 3 days:
6 hour buckets

3 days and <= 14 days:
1 day buckets

14 days and <= 60 days:
1 day or 6 hour buckets depending on datapoint volume

60 days:
1 week buckets

Make all bucket sizes configurable.

The output must include:

granularity
bucketStart
bucketEnd

==================================================
3. CLEAN RAW DATA

Before aggregation:

* sort timestamps
* remove null timestamps
* remove null values
* remove NaN
* remove infinite values
* handle duplicate timestamps deterministically
* do not fabricate missing datapoints
* preserve missing-data information

Return:

originalDataPointCount
validDataPointCount
invalidDataPointCount
missingDataPointCount

==================================================
4. BUCKET AGGREGATION

Group datapoints into time buckets.

For each bucket calculate appropriate statistics.

Every bucket must contain:

* bucketStart
* bucketEnd
* value
* minimum
* maximum
* average
* datapointCount

For COUNT metrics such as:

RequestCount
2xx
4xx
5xx

use SUM as the primary bucket value when configured.

For latency metrics such as:

ResponseTime
TargetResponseTime

use AVERAGE as the primary bucket value.

Also preserve minimum and maximum.

For utilization metrics such as:

CPU
Memory

use AVERAGE as the primary bucket value and preserve maximum.

Do not use one aggregation method for every metric.

Aggregation must be configurable per metric.

==================================================
5. BUCKET-TO-BUCKET CHANGE

For every bucket after the first, calculate:

previousValue
absoluteChange
percentageChange
direction

Direction:

INCREASED
DECREASED
UNCHANGED

Formula:

percentageChange =
((currentValue - previousValue) / abs(previousValue)) * 100

If previousValue == 0:

return null for percentageChange unless both values are zero.

==================================================
6. OVERALL TREND

Calculate the overall trend across the entire analysis period.

Possible values:

INCREASING
DECREASING
STABLE
FLUCTUATING
INSUFFICIENT_DATA

Use:

* linear regression slope
* bucket-to-bucket changes
* percentage change
* variability
* direction consistency

Do not determine trend only from firstValue and lastValue.

Example:

100 → 110 → 120 → 150 → 200

should be INCREASING.

Example:

100 → 150 → 100 → 150 → 100

should be FLUCTUATING.

==================================================
7. TREND PHASES

Identify meaningful phases in the time series.

Possible phases:

STABLE
GRADUAL_INCREASE
ACCELERATING_INCREASE
GRADUAL_DECREASE
ACCELERATING_DECREASE
SPIKE
DROP
ELEVATED
RECOVERING
FLUCTUATING

A phase must contain:

* start
* end
* phase type
* starting value
* ending value
* percentage change

Do not create a new phase for every small fluctuation.

==================================================
8. SPIKES

Detect significant spikes using a configurable rolling baseline.

Do not use only the global average.

For every significant spike return:

* timestamp/bucket
* value
* baseline
* deviationPercent
* duration if determinable

==================================================
9. DROPS

Detect significant downward anomalies using the same approach.

Return:

* timestamp/bucket
* value
* baseline
* deviationPercent
* duration

==================================================
10. THRESHOLD ANALYSIS

Use only externally supplied thresholds.

For every bucket determine whether threshold is breached.

Return:

* threshold
* operator
* breachCount
* firstBreach
* lastBreach
* maximumValue
* duration
* breachType

Breach types:

NONE
ISOLATED
REPEATED
SUSTAINED

Group consecutive threshold-breaching buckets into a single event.

==================================================
11. DERIVED METRICS

When required source metrics are available, calculate:

5xxRate =
5xx / RequestCount * 100

4xxRate =
4xx / RequestCount * 100

2xxRate =
2xx / RequestCount * 100

requestsPerHost =
RequestCount / HostCount

errorsPerHost =
(4xx + 5xx) / HostCount

Handle division by zero.

Derived metrics must also be converted into the same bucket structure and trend calculations.

==================================================
12. CROSS-METRIC ALIGNMENT

Align metrics using the same bucket boundaries.

For example:

10:00–10:05

RequestCount = 1000
5xx = 10
ResponseTime = 2.1

10:05–10:10

RequestCount = 1200
5xx = 20
ResponseTime = 2.8

This allows the downstream LLM to identify temporal relationships.

Calculate objective signals such as:

* correlation
* simultaneous increase
* simultaneous decrease
* simultaneous anomaly

Do not perform root-cause analysis.

==================================================
13. REGIONAL INFORMATION

Never remove:

* region
* service
* resource
* CloudWatch dimensions

The downstream LLM must be able to determine WHERE a trend or event occurred.

==================================================
14. IMPORTANT EVENTS

Preserve exact timestamps for:

* spikes
* drops
* threshold breaches
* trend changes
* recovery

Do not lose exact event timestamps when aggregating.

==================================================
